  <p class="lead">
    Most "free" PDF tools have a catch: they upload your files to a server, process them there, and send the result back. The privacy policy says files are deleted after an hour. You have no way to verify that. For a personal photo, this trade-off is fine. For a contract, a medical record, or an HR document — it is a real risk you should know about before hitting upload.
  </p>
  <p>
    Browser-based PDF tools are different. They run the processing code directly in your browser tab using JavaScript and native browser APIs. Your file never travels across the network. The computation happens on your own CPU. You can verify this yourself: open DevTools → Network tab, then run the tool. The tab stays quiet.
  </p>
  <p>
    This article covers what browser-based PDF tools can genuinely do in 2026, which tools do it well, and where the limits are.
  </p>

  <h2>What browser-based PDF tools can do today</h2>
  <p>
    The assumption that serious PDF work requires a server was true in 2015. It is no longer true. Modern browsers expose a full set of APIs that make client-side PDF processing practical:
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>pdf-lib</strong> — a pure JavaScript library that reads and writes PDF documents. Handles merging, splitting, rotating, reordering, watermarking, adding page numbers, deleting pages, and PDF compression — including, as of a recent update, recompressing the embedded photos that usually make a PDF large, not just stripping metadata.</li>
    <li><strong>pdf.js</strong> — Mozilla's PDF rendering engine, the same one inside Firefox. Renders PDF pages to canvas at any resolution. Powers PDF-to-JPG and PDF-to-PNG conversion. Also extracts text content for PDF-to-text tools.</li>
    <li><strong>Canvas API + FileReader</strong> — handles all image processing: resize, crop, rotate, flip, grayscale, format conversion, Base64 encoding, and (via the browser's own JPEG encoder) recompressing photos at a lower quality.</li>
    <li><strong>WebAssembly</strong> — lets compiled C/C++ code run in the browser at near-native speed. Some browser-based tools use it for heavier decoding work (HEIC photo decoding is a common case); plenty of the operations above run in plain JavaScript instead.</li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    The result: the large majority of everyday PDF work can now run entirely client-side, including the "make this smaller" job people usually assume needs a server. The parts that genuinely still don't: PDF-to-Word conversion, OCR, and true multi-pass Ghostscript-level compression for extreme cases. I built keptlocal around that split: local-first for everything that can be local, and honest about the boundary where it can't.
  </p>

